Given, $R_{1}=100 \Omega, R_{2}=R_{3}=50 \Omega, E=6 \mathrm{ V} ,
$R_{4}=75 \Omega$
As $R_{2}, R_{3} and R_{4}$ are in parallel combination, so their equivalent resistance be
 \begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{R^{\prime}} &=\frac{1}{R_{2}}+\frac{1}{R_{3}}+\frac{1}{R_{4}} \\
&=\frac{1}{50}+\frac{1}{50}+\frac{1}{75}=\frac{3+3+2}{150}=\frac{8}{150}
\end{aligned}
or R^{\prime}= \frac{150}{8}$  \Omega$
Now, $R^{\prime}$ is in series with $R_{1}$, so
$R_{$\text{net } }=R_{1}+R^{\prime}=100+\frac{150}{8}=\frac{950}{8}=118.75 \Omega$

