Suppose, the length of wire is $l$. When, wire formed in circular loop then radius of loop, r=$\frac{l}{2 \pi}[\because l=2 \pi r]$
Magnet dipole, $M_{1}=i A $\begin{array}{l}
=i \pi r^{2}=i \pi \times $\left(\frac{l}{2 \pi}$\right)^{2}$\\
=i \times \frac{l^{2}}{4 \pi}
\end{array}$ When, wire formed in square loop then the side of loop, a=$\frac{l}{4}$ Magnet dipole, $M_{2}=I A \begin{aligned}$&=i $\times$ a^{2} \\
&=i $\times \frac{l^{2}}{16}$\\
$\frac{M_{1}}{M_{2}}$&=$\frac{4}{\pi}$
\end{aligned}
